#1 Tables and structured data

Tables are block nodes rather than inline formatting. StructMark separates simple pipe tables from CSV, TSV and JSON structured tables.

#2 Simple tables

Pipe tables define their header and separator row explicitly. Pipes within inline code require appropriate parsing.

```sm
| Syntax | Category |
| --- | --- |
| *[Strong] | Inline |
| &note | Directive |
```

| Syntax | Category |
| --- | --- |
| *[Strong] | Inline |
| &note | Directive |

#2 CSV and TSV

The explicit `format` attribute selects the parser. Invalid column counts should produce diagnostics.

```sm
&table[format=csv]
Name,Version
StructMark,1.1
&end
```

#2 JSON structured tables

Use structured data for spans and grid occupancy; invalid spans, overlaps and missing cells should be diagnosed.

```sm
&table[format=json]
{"columns":["Name","Version"],"rows":[["StructMark","1.1"]]}
&end
```

#2 Markdown conversion

Markdown supports only a compatible subset. Pipe characters, multiline cells and unsupported semantics require care during export.
